GIVEN `the proceedings are proceedings under section 164.1` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.1(8) subsections 164(6) and (7) apply to the proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings are proceedings under section 164.1`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Subsections 164(6) and (7) apply, with any modifications that the circumstances require, to this section"

-- (9) "No order made under subsections (5) to (7) takes effect until the time for final appeal has expired": an "until" bar, encoded as the bar (ruling (d) of 2026-11-05): JUST TRUE before the time has expired, JUST FALSE after.
-- src:3328 | When order takes effect
-- src:3329 | (9) No order made under subsections (5) to (7) takes effect until the time for final appeal has expired.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.1(9) (src:3328-3329)
GIVEN `the order is made under subsection (5), (6) or (7)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the time for final appeal has expired` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.1(9) the order cannot yet take effect` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the order is made under subsection (5), (6) or (7)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "No order made under subsections (5) to (7) takes effect until the time for final appeal has expired"
        IF `the time for final appeal has expired` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "until the time for final appeal has expired"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `s 164.1(10) the term` IS ONE OF
    `s 164.1(10) computer data`
    `s 164.1(10) computer system`
    `s 164.1(10) court`
    `s 164.1(10) illicit material`
    `s 164.1(10) judge`
    `s 164.1(10) another term`

-- (10) "computer data and computer system have the same meanings as in subsection 342.1(2) and court, illicit material and judge have the same meanings as in subsection 164(8)": a cross-reference.
-- The function says which section gives the term its meaning (JUST TRUE for the five terms; NOTHING for another term); the meaning of a term of s 342.1(2) is in another part (an input of the part that owns it).
-- src:3330 | Definitions
-- src:3331 | (10) In this section, computer data and computer system have the same meanings as in subsection 342.1(2) and court, illicit material and judge have the same meanings as in subsection 164(8).
@ref Criminal Code s 164.1(10) (src:3330-3331)
GIVEN term IS A `s 164.1(10) the term`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.1(10) the term has the meaning given by subsection 342.1(2) or subsection 164(8)` MEANS
    CONSIDER term
    WHEN `s 164.1(10) another term` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "computer data and computer system have the same meanings as in subsection 342.1(2) and court, illicit material and judge have the same meanings as in subsection 164(8)"
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `s 164.2 Forfeiture after conviction`

-- (1) "On application of the Attorney General, a court that convicts a person of an offence under section 162.1, 163.1, 172.1 or 172.2, in addition to any other punishment that it may impose, may order that anything — other than real property — be forfeited ... if it is satisfied, on a balance of probabilities, that the thing (a) was used in the commission of the offence; and (b) is the property of (i) ... or (ii) ...".
-- src:3332 | Forfeiture after conviction
-- src:3333 | 164.2 (1) On application of the Attorney General, a court that convicts a person of an offence under section 162.1, 163.1, 172.1 or 172.2, in addition to any other punishment that it may impose, may order that anything — other than real property — be forfeited to Her Majesty and disposed of as the Attorney General directs if it is satisfied, on a balance of probabilities, that the thing
-- src:3334 | (a) was used in the commission of the offence; and
-- src:3335 | (b) is the property of
-- src:3336 | (i) the convicted person or another person who was a party to the offence, or
-- src:3337 | (ii) a person who acquired the thing from a person referred to in subparagraph (i) under circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred for the purpose of avoiding forfeiture.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.2(1) (src:3332-3337)
-- LIMB BY LIMB (sweep of 2026-11-12, fork F9): the application limb is a gate of the upon kind, so a failed application limb answers as the guide does (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05): JUST FALSE, listed in the fork register with the other reading (NOTHING). The other limbs are if-gates or scope limits and answer NOTHING (11-12 limit (1); ruling (c) of 2026-11-05).
GIVEN `the Attorney General applied` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence of which the person is convicted` IS A `Provision of the Code`
      `the thing is real property` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied, on a balance of probabilities, that the thing was used in the commission of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing is the property of the convicted person or of another person who was a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing is the property of a person who acquired it from such a person under circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred for the purpose of avoiding forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.2(1) the court may order the thing forfeited to Her Majesty` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the Attorney General applied`
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "On application of the Attorney General"
        IF    (   `ss 150 to 182 the provision is in the section` `the offence of which the person is convicted` 162.1
               OR `ss 150 to 182 the provision is in the section` `the offence of which the person is convicted` 163.1
               OR `ss 150 to 182 the provision is in the section` `the offence of which the person is convicted` 172.1
               OR `ss 150 to 182 the provision is in the section` `the offence of which the person is convicted` 172.2)
          AND (NOT `the thing is real property`)
          AND `the court is satisfied, on a balance of probabilities, that the thing was used in the commission of the offence`
          AND (   `the thing is the property of the convicted person or of another person who was a party to the offence`
               OR `the thing is the property of a person who acquired it from such a person under circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred for the purpose of avoiding forfeiture`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "On application of the Attorney General, a court that convicts a person of an offence under section 162.1, 163.1, 172.1 or 172.2, in addition to any other punishment that it may impose, may order that anything — other than real property — be forfeited to Her Majesty and disposed of as the Attorney General directs if it is satisfied, on a balance of probabilities, that the thing"

-- src:3338 | Third party rights
-- src:3339 | (2) Before making an order under subsection (1), the court shall cause notice to be given to, and may hear, any person whom it considers to have an interest in the thing, and may declare the nature and extent of the person’s interest in it.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.2(2) (src:3338-3339)
GIVEN `the court is about to make an order under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court considers the person to have an interest in the thing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.2(2) the court must cause notice to be given to the person` MEANS
    IF `the court is about to make an order under subsection (1)` AND `the court considers the person to have an interest in the thing`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Before making an order under subsection (1), the court shall cause notice to be given to, and may hear, any person whom it considers to have an interest in the thing"

@ref Criminal Code s 164.2(2) (src:3339)
GIVEN `the court considers the person to have an interest in the thing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.2(2) the court may hear the person and declare the nature and extent of the person's interest` MEANS
    IF `the court considers the person to have an interest in the thing`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may hear, any person whom it considers to have an interest in the thing, and may declare the nature and extent of the person’s interest in it"

-- src:3340 | Right of appeal — third party
-- src:3341 | (3) A person who was heard in response to a notice given under subsection (2) may appeal to the court of appeal against an order made under subsection (1).
@ref Criminal Code s 164.2(3) (src:3340-3341)
GIVEN `the person was heard in response to a notice given under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the appeal is against an order made under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.2(3) the person may appeal to the court of appeal` MEANS
    IF `the person was heard in response to a notice given under subsection (2)` AND `the appeal is against an order made under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A person who was heard in response to a notice given under subsection (2) may appeal to the court of appeal against an order made under subsection (1)"

-- src:3342 | Right of appeal — Attorney General
-- src:3343 | (4) The Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the refusal of a court to make an order under subsection (1).
@ref Criminal Code s 164.2(4) (src:3342-3343)
GIVEN `the court refused to make an order under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.2(4) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal` MEANS
    IF `the court refused to make an order under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the refusal of a court to make an order under subsection (1)"

-- src:3344 | Application of Part XXI
-- src:3345 | (5) Part XXI applies, with any modifications that the circumstances require, with respect to the procedure for an appeal under subsections (3) and (4).
@ref Criminal Code s 164.2(5) (src:3344-3345)
GIVEN `the appeal is an appeal under subsection (3) or (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.2(5) Part XXI applies to the appeal` MEANS
    IF `the appeal is an appeal under subsection (3) or (4)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Part XXI applies, with any modifications that the circumstances require, with respect to the procedure for an appeal under subsections (3) and (4)"

§ `s 164.3 Relief from forfeiture`

DECLARE `s 164.3(1) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 164.3(1) apply in writing to a judge for an order under subsection (4)`

-- (1) "Within thirty days after an order under subsection 164.2(1) is made, a person who claims an interest in the thing forfeited may apply in writing to a judge for an order under subsection (4)."
-- The period is fixed in the text's own words: an application after the thirtieth day is JUST FALSE (ruling (a)); a person who claims no interest, or an application not in writing, is outside the grant: NOTHING.
-- src:3346 | Relief from forfeiture
-- src:3347 | 164.3 (1) Within thirty days after an order under subsection 164.2(1) is made, a person who claims an interest in the thing forfeited may apply in writing to a judge for an order under subsection (4).
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(1) (src:3346-3347)
GIVEN `the person claims an interest in the thing forfeited` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the application is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the number of days from the day on which the order under subsection 164.2(1) was made to the day of the application` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.3(1) the person may apply for relief from forfeiture` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the person claims an interest in the thing forfeited` AND `the application is in writing`)
        THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a person who claims an interest in the thing forfeited may apply in writing to a judge"
        IF `the number of days from the day on which the order under subsection 164.2(1) was made to the day of the application` LESS THAN 0 THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): an application made before the order is not a case the text speaks to
        IF `the number of days from the day on which the order under subsection 164.2(1) was made to the day of the application` AT MOST 30 THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Within thirty days after an order under subsection 164.2(1) is made"

@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(1) (src:3347)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 164 to 164.3 party` `s 164.3(1) act`
`s 164.3(1) the claimant's permission to apply` MEANS
    PARTY `ss 164 to 164.3 a person who claims an interest in the thing forfeited`
    MAY `s 164.3(1) apply in writing to a judge for an order under subsection (4)`
    WITHIN 30
    HENCE FULFILLED

-- (2) "The judge shall fix a day — not less than thirty days after the application is made — for its hearing."
-- src:3348 | Hearing of application
-- src:3349 | (2) The judge shall fix a day — not less than thirty days after the application is made — for its hearing.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(2) (src:3348-3349)
GIVEN `the number of days from the day the application is made to the day fixed for its hearing` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.3(2) the day fixed for the hearing is one the judge may fix` MEANS
    IF `the number of days from the day the application is made to the day fixed for its hearing` AT LEAST 30
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The judge shall fix a day — not less than thirty days after the application is made — for its hearing"

DECLARE `s 164.3(3) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 164.3(3) cause notice of the application and of the hearing day to be served on the Attorney General`

-- (3) "At least fifteen days before the hearing, the applicant shall cause notice of the application and of the hearing day to be served on the Attorney General."
-- src:3350 | Notice to Attorney General
-- src:3351 | (3) At least fifteen days before the hearing, the applicant shall cause notice of the application and of the hearing day to be served on the Attorney General.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(3) (src:3350-3351)
GIVEN `the number of days from the day notice was served on the Attorney General to the day of the hearing` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.3(3) the applicant served notice on the Attorney General at least fifteen days before the hearing` MEANS
    IF `the number of days from the day notice was served on the Attorney General to the day of the hearing` AT LEAST 15
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "At least fifteen days before the hearing, the applicant shall cause notice of the application and of the hearing day to be served on the Attorney General"

-- The deadline is the hearing day less 15 days, counted from the day of the application (the unit is a day).
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(3) (src:3351)
GIVEN `the number of days from the day of the application to the day of the hearing` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 164 to 164.3 party` `s 164.3(3) act`
`s 164.3(3) the applicant's duty to serve notice on the Attorney General` MEANS
    PARTY `ss 164 to 164.3 the applicant for relief from forfeiture`
    MUST `s 164.3(3) cause notice of the application and of the hearing day to be served on the Attorney General`
    WITHIN (`the number of days from the day of the application to the day of the hearing` MINUS 15)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `ss 164 to 164.3 the applicant for relief from forfeiture` BECAUSE "the applicant did not cause notice to be served on the Attorney General at least fifteen days before the hearing"

-- (4) "The judge may make an order declaring that the applicant's interest in the thing is not affected by the forfeiture ... if the judge is satisfied that the applicant (a) was not a party to the offence; and (b) did not acquire the thing from a person who was a party to the offence under circumstances ...": a power, an "if" gate.
-- src:3352 | Order
-- src:3353 | (4) The judge may make an order declaring that the applicant’s interest in the thing is not affected by the forfeiture and declaring the nature and extent of the interest if the judge is satisfied that the applicant
-- src:3354 | (a) was not a party to the offence; and
-- src:3355 | (b) did not acquire the thing from a person who was a party to the offence under circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred for the purpose of avoiding forfeiture.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(4) (src:3352-3355)
GIVEN `the judge is satisfied that the applicant was not a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judge is satisfied that the applicant did not acquire the thing from a person who was a party to the offence under circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred for the purpose of avoiding forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.3(4) the judge may declare that the applicant's interest in the thing is not affected by the forfeiture` MEANS
    IF    `the judge is satisfied that the applicant was not a party to the offence`
      AND `the judge is satisfied that the applicant did not acquire the thing from a person who was a party to the offence under circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred for the purpose of avoiding forfeiture`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The judge may make an order declaring that the applicant’s interest in the thing is not affected by the forfeiture and declaring the nature and extent of the interest if the judge is satisfied that the applicant (a) was not a party to the offence; and (b) did not acquire the thing from a person who was a party to the offence"

-- src:3356 | Appeal to court of appeal
-- src:3357 | (5) A person referred to in subsection (4) or the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against an order made under that subsection. Part XXI applies, with any modifications that the circumstances require, with respect to the procedure for an appeal under this subsection.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(5) (src:3356-3357)
GIVEN `the person is the person referred to in subsection (4) or the Attorney General` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the appeal is against an order made under subsection (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.3(5) the person may appeal to the court of appeal` MEANS
    IF `the person is the person referred to in subsection (4) or the Attorney General` AND `the appeal is against an order made under subsection (4)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A person referred to in subsection (4) or the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against an order made under that subsection"

-- (6) "On application by a person who obtained an order under subsection (4), made after the expiration of the time allowed for an appeal against the order and, if an appeal is taken, after it has been finally disposed of, the Attorney General shall direct that (a) the thing be returned to the person; or (b) an amount equal to the value of the extent of the person's interest ... be paid to the person."
-- The text does not say which of (a) and (b) the Attorney General is to direct, or when: the function answers only whether the duty to direct one of the two has arisen.
-- src:3358 | Powers of Attorney General
-- src:3359 | (6) On application by a person who obtained an order under subsection (4), made after the expiration of the time allowed for an appeal against the order and, if an appeal is taken, after it has been finally disposed of, the Attorney General shall direct that
-- src:3360 | (a) the thing be returned to the person; or
-- src:3361 | (b) an amount equal to the value of the extent of the person’s interest, as declared in the order, be paid to the person.
@ref Criminal Code s 164.3(6) (src:3358-3361)
GIVEN `the person obtained an order under subsection (4) and applied` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the time allowed for an appeal against the order has expired` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an appeal was taken` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the appeal has been finally disposed of` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164.3(6) the Attorney General must direct that the thing be returned or its value paid` MEANS
    IF    `the person obtained an order under subsection (4) and applied`
      AND `the time allowed for an appeal against the order has expired`
      AND (   (NOT `an appeal was taken`)
           OR `the appeal has been finally disposed of`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "On application by a person who obtained an order under subsection (4), made after the expiration of the time allowed for an appeal against the order and, if an appeal is taken, after it has been finally disposed of, the Attorney General shall direct that"
